  "self_critique": "The run found a modest improvement, but the search was shallow and poorly identified.\n\nWhat was suboptimal\n- Node_001 bundled architecture, objective mix, optimizer, schedule, dropout, and recency weighting into one experiment. The +0.0014 gain cannot be attributed to any component, making the result hard to reproduce or extend.\n- Its hypothesis claimed an expected +0.0029, but achieved roughly +0.0014. The direction was right, but the estimate was overconfident.\n- The run recognized missing learning-curve telemetry only after making an overfitting diagnosis. That diagnosis was not adequately supported.\n- Node_002 reported a higher rounded primary (0.6039) than the accepted best (0.603179) yet was rejected. The journal should record the exact rejection reason\u2014variance, leakage/causality violation, secondary constraints, or evaluation failure. As written, the outcome is internally confusing.\n- Only two evaluable challengers were attempted, and node_003 failed at proposal generation. Declaring convergence after this is premature; it looks more like search exhaustion or scaffold failure.\n- The baseline sigma of 0.0001 appears implausibly precise unless it came from multiple independent seeds or folds. Uncertainty methodology is not documented.\n\nScaffold changes\n- Require complete train/validation curves, exact metrics, seed counts, uncertainty estimates, and rejection reasons in every node.\n- Enforce one-factor or small-factor experiments after a bundled winner: ablate each package component before adding new features.\n- Separate hypothesis generation from executable-spec validation so malformed proposals are repaired rather than consuming a search node.\n- Distinguish \u201cconverged\u201d from \u201cbudget exhausted,\u201d \u201cproposal failed,\u201d and \u201cno statistically reliable improvement.\u201d\n- Add automatic leakage and temporal-causality checks for session and context features.\n- Calibrate predicted effect sizes from prior deltas instead of allowing unsupported point estimates.\n\nWhat the next run should try first\n1. Re-run node_001 across several seeds and confirm the gain against node_000 under identical evaluation.\n2. Ablate node_001\u2019s components, starting with recency weighting and the DCN-lite architecture; then test BPR/logloss mixing and optimizer/schedule changes separately.\n3. If the gain survives, retry node_002\u2019s strictly causal temporal/session features one family at a time, with explicit rejection diagnostics and temporal leakage checks.\n4. Preserve node_001 as the incumbent, but do not treat the current run as genuine convergence until reproducibility and attribution are established."
